
# 2026-10-26 23:08:41.188903
+/ask You are reviewing the sync code of 4ENote, an Android note app. Find REAL BUGS, especially ones that LOSE OR CORRUPT USER DATA. Ignore style.
+
+Design: each device writes only its own cloud file per note, "<noteId>.<deviceId>.4enote" (old single files "<noteId>.4enote" are read as peer "legacy", never written). A device merges other devices' changed files three-way (NoteMerger) with a base = union of everything both sides had seen; each file records "seen" = {deviceId: version} of files merged into it; each device keeps the other devices' files as last merged (peers/) and its own last 3 uploads (sent/). Note.rev is an edit counter (dirty upload = rev+1) deciding single-value conflicts; ties go to the smaller device id via remoteFirst. Strokes are immutable with ids; an edited stroke records `replaces`. A note is deleted elsewhere when all its files are gone and it was synced (isInCloud) and not dirty. Cloud versions: Drive md5, WebDAV ETag, local folder SHA-256.
+
+Look hard at: concurrency of two devices syncing at the same moment; crashes or partial failures between steps (upload done but markSynced not, savePeer order); editor saving while sync runs (replaceIfUnchanged / expectedUpdatedAt); open notes; locked/encrypted notes; the first sync after updating from the old single-file format; deletion and trash; mergeById ordering and oneEditPerStroke; ping-pong uploads that never stop; memory with very large notes; untrusted cloud input.
+
+For each bug give: file + function, a CONCRETE sequence of events that triggers it, what goes wrong for the user, how sure you are (high/medium/low), and a fix. List the most severe first. Say clearly if you find nothing serious. Do not invent bugs; quote the code you rely on.

